      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(0)=5,\\,{f}^{′}(0)=-2,\\,g(0)=10,\\,{g}^{′}(0)=4\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))=f(x){g}^{′}(x)+g(x){f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=0,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=0} & =f(0){g}^{′}(0)+g(0){f}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4+10\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
